From u15f73b03d7264ad1 at groups.google.com  Mon Jul 29 10:35:56 2019
From: u15f73b03d7264ad1 at groups.google.com (Ben Temperton)
Date: Mon, 29 Jul 2019 10:35:56 +0000
Subject: plot all sample lines in an ecdf, but color by treatment
Message-ID: <uLov2VtbgkU.g4VugyQFEQAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/uLov2VtbgkU/m/g4VugyQFEQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi All,

I have a large dataset where I am trying to compare the distributions between treatments using ECDF. In this toy example, I have two treatments, each with two samples. I want to plot all 4 sample ECDFs on a single plot, but colored by treatment. I can get it to color the lines by sample (giving 4 colored lines), OR I can get it to plot by treatment (giving 2 colored lines). What I can't figure out is how to plot all four lines, but color them by treatment:

This gives me the nearest to what I want (but with 4 colors):
library(ggplot2)

test_df = data.frame(length=c(rnorm(1000, mean=500, sd=100),
                              rnorm(1000, mean=501, sd=100),
                              rnorm(1000, mean=200, sd=100),
                              rnorm(1000, mean=201, sd=100)),
                     treatment=c(rep('treatment.1', 1000),
                               rep('treatment.1', 1000),
                               rep('treatment.2', 1000),
                               rep('treatment.2', 1000)),
                     sample=c(rep('sample.1', 1000),
                              rep('sample.2', 1000),
                              rep('sample.3', 1000),
                              rep('sample.4', 1000)))

ggplot(test_df, aes(x=length, y = ..y.., color=sample)) +
  stat_ecdf(geom = "step", size = 0.75) +
  scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0, 0), name= "Length") +
  scale_y_continuous(limits = c(-.01, 1.01), expand = c(0, 0), name = "cumulative frequency")

How do I get all four lines to plot, but colored by treatment?

Thanks!
Ben

From ude9071e8091a07a9 at groups.google.com  Mon Jul 29 10:55:12 2019
From: ude9071e8091a07a9 at groups.google.com (Lorenzo Crippa)
Date: Mon, 29 Jul 2019 10:55:12 +0000
Subject: Re: plot all sample lines in an ecdf, but color by treatment
Message-ID: <uLov2VtbgkU.dtkdcTEGEQAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <uLov2VtbgkU.g4VugyQFEQAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <uLov2VtbgkU.g4VugyQFEQAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/uLov2VtbgkU/m/dtkdcTEGEQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear Ben,

I think I don’t understand what your goal is. If you simply want to plot the four lines (corresponding to the four samples) coloured by treatment (thus, two will have one colour, and the other two will have a different one), you might simply type:

ggplot(test_df, aes(x=length, y = ..y.., group=sample, color=treatment)) +

  stat_ecdf(geom = "step", size = 0.75) +
  scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0, 0), name= "Length") +
  scale_y_continuous(limits = c(-.01, 1.01), expand = c(0, 0), name = "cumulative frequency")

Yet, this would make it impossible to distinguish what line refers to the first vs the second and what line refers to the third vs the fourth sample.

Alternatively, you could differentiate the four samples by line type and the two treatments by colour:

ggplot(test_df, aes(x=length, y = ..y.., linetype=sample, color=treatment)) +

  stat_ecdf(geom = "step", size = 0.75) +
  scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0, 0), name= "Length") +
  scale_y_continuous(limits = c(-.01, 1.01), expand = c(0, 0), name = "cumulative frequency")

This would let the viewer tell what line refers to a given sample.

All the best,
Lorenzo

